# KEYMAP.md §Board and card detail (`[` / `]`) · UX-SPEC.md §Board — `]` moves the focused card
# to the next column and the cursor follows it there.
#
# Moving a card is the board's only destructive-ish key that has no confirm, so what it does has
# to be exactly what it says: one card changes status, the counts on both columns change by one,
# and the cursor stays on the card the user was holding rather than on the slot it left. `[`
# undoes it by the same rule, which is the property that makes a mis-keyed `]` recoverable.
#
# The per-column counts in `lists.board.rows[N].badges[0]` are the oracle, and the card list
# itself is `lists["board.cards"]` — the name carries a dot, so it needs the quoted path step
# the predicate grammar spells `["…"]` (TESTING-HARNESS.md §2).
#
# Headless: virtual-lane only, because of its `shot`. The keyboard does reach the focused view
# in the headless lane (§7), so everything else here would run there.
fixture: board
await idle 20000
key g b
await hub_tab == Board 10000
assert lists.board.rows[0].badges[0] == "2" && lists.board.rows[1].badges[0] == "2"
assert focused == "board.column[0].card[0]"
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[0] exists

# `]` moves `FLT-1` from Backlog to Todo. The counts are the proof it really changed status.
key ]
await lists.board.rows[0].badges[0] == "1" 10000
assert lists.board.rows[1].badges[0] == "3"

# The cursor followed the card into its new column instead of staying behind.
assert lists.board.selected.label == "Todo" && focused == "board.column[1].card[2]"
shot moved

# `[` puts it back, so a mis-keyed move costs one keystroke.
key [
await lists.board.rows[0].badges[0] == "2" 10000
assert lists.board.rows[1].badges[0] == "2" && lists.board.selected.label == "Backlog"
dump moved-back
quit
